WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:07.600 align:start position:0%
As the Middle Ages gave way to the early
modern period, mercenaries became a

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:11.200 align:start position:0%
central part of European armies. Their
growing importance brought profound

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:16.100 align:start position:0%
changes to the ways wars were fought,
changes that would last for centuries.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:20.700 align:start position:0%
The use of professional soldiers in
itself was nothing new. Until then,

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:25.000 align:start position:0%
however, they had generally served in
smaller numbers as specialists or

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:28.200 align:start position:0%
powerful reinforcements, such as the
famous great companies of the Hundred

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:32.900 align:start position:0%
Years' War.
From the late 15th century onward, that

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:38.000 align:start position:0%
began to change. Mercenaries made up a
growing share of European forces until

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:41.200 align:start position:0%
entire mercenary armies were fighting
Europe's wars.

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:45.400 align:start position:0%
In this video, we'll look at how
historians explain the rise of

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:49.200 align:start position:0%
mercenaries and the role these soldiers
actually played in warfare.

00:00:50.435 --> 00:00:52.455 align:start position:0%
[music]

00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:58.200 align:start position:0%
The rise of mercenary armies was closely
tied to military developments in the

00:00:56.500 --> 00:01:02.500 align:start position:0%
15th century.
We've covered many of these developments

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:06.700 align:start position:0%
in earlier videos, so here we'll stick
to the essentials and revisit just

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:09.900 align:start position:0%
enough to make the argument clear.
But first, we need to go back a little

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:15.100 align:start position:0%
further.
In the 12th and 13th centuries, heavy

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:19.800 align:start position:0%
cavalry dominated Europe's battlefields.
A powerful charge could shatter an enemy

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:24.200 align:start position:0%
formation in moments and decide the
outcome of a battle.

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:27.900 align:start position:0%
Armies made up entirely of infantry
found it especially difficult to fight

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:32.200 align:start position:0%
mounted troops.
But that doesn't mean they stood no

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:36.600 align:start position:0%
chance. There were repeated examples of
foot soldiers successfully holding their

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:41.300 align:start position:0%
ground against mounted troops, including
in the famous battles such as

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:44.400 align:start position:0%
Bannockburn, Crécy, Agincourt, and
Courtrai.

00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:47.900 align:start position:0%
These successes all had something in
common, though.

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:52.800 align:start position:0%
The infantry fought on the defensive,
supported by field fortifications, a

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:57.900 align:start position:0%
wagon fort, large numbers of archers, or
favorable terrain.

00:01:54.500 --> 00:02:03.292 align:start position:0%
At Courtrai in 1302, for example, a
Flemish army dug pits and trenches in

00:02:00.592 --> 00:02:06.992 align:start position:0%
marshy ground between the rivers.
Because of these obstacles, the French

00:02:05.092 --> 00:02:12.492 align:start position:0%
men-at-arms were unable to bring the
full force of their charge to bear and

00:02:09.292 --> 00:02:17.192 align:start position:0%
suffered a crushing defeat.
In the 14th and 15th centuries, a more

00:02:15.192 --> 00:02:20.792 align:start position:0%
reliable answer to heavy cavalry
emerged.

00:02:18.492 --> 00:02:24.992 align:start position:0%
A new type of fighting force built
around compact infantry formations armed

00:02:23.292 --> 00:02:28.692 align:start position:0%
with long pikes.
Traditionally, historians have traced

00:02:27.092 --> 00:02:33.092 align:start position:0%
the rise of these pike squares to the
Old Swiss Confederacy, although the

00:02:30.892 --> 00:02:38.392 align:start position:0%
Scots had developed a similar solution,
the schiltron, a good 100 years earlier.

00:02:36.392 --> 00:02:42.992 align:start position:0%
In any case, the Swiss fought several
wars in quick succession during the 15th

00:02:40.692 --> 00:02:48.092 align:start position:0%
century, which meant that they had an
unusually large number of experienced,

00:02:45.392 --> 00:02:51.892 align:start position:0%
battle-hardened men to draw on.
Their experience allowed these veterans

00:02:50.092 --> 00:02:56.592 align:start position:0%
to coordinate their movements and fight
effectively in large formations.

00:02:54.592 --> 00:03:00.792 align:start position:0%
This was the basis of the Swiss recipe
for success, the so-called

00:02:59.692 --> 00:03:04.992 align:start position:0%
These
were formations of heavy infantry drawn

00:03:02.892 --> 00:03:09.392 align:start position:0%
up many ranks deep that relied on the
momentum of mass of men.

00:03:07.292 --> 00:03:14.492 align:start position:0%
Soldiers along the edges carried pikes
up to 5 m long so that to break the

00:03:12.092 --> 00:03:17.592 align:start position:0%
formation, attackers had to force their
way through a hatch of hundreds of steel

00:03:16.392 --> 00:03:21.392 align:start position:0%
points.
Even heavily armored men-at-arms

00:03:19.592 --> 00:03:25.792 align:start position:0%
struggled against them, as the
Burgundian wars clearly demonstrated.

00:03:24.192 --> 00:03:29.592 align:start position:0%
And the Swiss pike formations did even
more than to hold their ground against

00:03:27.692 --> 00:03:34.892 align:start position:0%
Burgundian heavy cavalry.
They also attacked successfully. That

00:03:32.292 --> 00:03:39.692 align:start position:0%
had almost never happened before.
The most famous of these victories came

00:03:36.492 --> 00:03:44.192 align:start position:0%
at the Battle of Murten in 1476.
There, the Swiss and their allies caught

00:03:41.792 --> 00:03:48.092 align:start position:0%
Charles the Bold of Burgundy, of Guard,
surrounded his army and inflicted a

00:03:46.292 --> 00:03:51.692 align:start position:0%
devastating defeat.
This victory sent shock waves across

00:03:50.492 --> 00:03:55.592 align:start position:0%
Europe.
By defeating one of the most powerful

00:03:53.492 --> 00:03:59.892 align:start position:0%
armies of the day, they had shown just
what their infantry could do.

00:03:57.892 --> 00:04:02.894 align:start position:0%
Europe's rulers were impressed, [music]
and they wanted troops like this for

00:04:01.494 --> 00:04:07.694 align:start position:0%
themselves.
Swiss soldiers soon became highly sought

00:04:05.394 --> 00:04:11.994 align:start position:0%
after on the mercenary market, and the
Swiss Confederacy became one of the

00:04:09.394 --> 00:04:14.594 align:start position:0%
major sources of mercenary manpower in
Europe.

00:04:13.094 --> 00:04:18.294 align:start position:0%
Among their best customers were the
French kings.

00:04:16.194 --> 00:04:25.894 align:start position:0%
Louis XI established a corps of Swiss
soldiers as early as 1480, and in 1497,

00:04:23.294 --> 00:04:31.194 align:start position:0%
Charles VIII created the Cent-Suisses,
an elite bodyguard of Swiss mercenaries

00:04:28.094 --> 00:04:36.994 align:start position:0%
that served until 1792 and, after being
restored, until 1830. Spain, Savoy, and

00:04:34.994 --> 00:04:41.494 align:start position:0%
the Habsburgs also began employing more
troops from the Confederacy.

00:04:39.794 --> 00:04:46.094 align:start position:0%
Swiss victories did more than increase
demand for Swiss mercenaries.

00:04:43.694 --> 00:04:51.694 align:start position:0%
They also inspired imitators.
After the Burgundian Wars, Maximilian I

00:04:48.794 --> 00:04:57.294 align:start position:0%
of Habsburg, the emperor's son and heir
to Burgundy, began raising his own force

00:04:54.394 --> 00:05:01.094 align:start position:0%
on the Swiss model, the Landsknechts.
After some expert imitation, he had an

00:04:59.294 --> 00:05:05.294 align:start position:0%
effective infantry force by the early
16th century, one that increasingly

00:05:03.494 --> 00:05:10.294 align:start position:0%
rivaled the Swiss both on the
battlefield and the mercenary market.

00:05:08.194 --> 00:05:14.394 align:start position:0%
The Landsknechts also fought in pike
squares, but their formations differed

00:05:12.494 --> 00:05:18.694 align:start position:0%
from the Swiss Gewalthaufen.
They were arranged in alternating ranks

00:05:16.494 --> 00:05:22.894 align:start position:0%
of pikemen and soldiers carrying shorter
weapons. The striking success [music]

00:05:20.894 --> 00:05:26.894 align:start position:0%
of both the Swiss and the Landsknechts
around the turn of the century convinced

00:05:24.894 --> 00:05:30.894 align:start position:0%
rulers all over Europe that they needed
troops of this kind.

00:05:28.794 --> 00:05:35.094 align:start position:0%
The fact that both groups fought in much
the same way only reinforced that

00:05:33.294 --> 00:05:38.894 align:start position:0%
conclusion.
Demand for infantry formations kept

00:05:36.994 --> 00:05:43.794 align:start position:0%
growing, and infantry made up an
ever-larger share of European armies.

00:05:42.094 --> 00:05:48.994 align:start position:0%
But one duke was about to learn the hard
way what it means when your army is

00:05:45.494 --> 00:05:54.794 align:start position:0%
mostly hired. At Novara in 1500, his
soldiers simply refused to fight.

00:05:52.094 --> 00:05:58.694 align:start position:0%
More on that in a moment. First, a word
from today's sponsor. This video is

