Italy.

00:07:03.595 --> 00:07:10.895 align:start position:0%
The scale of this change became clear in
the Italian wars. From 1494 onward,

00:07:08.895 --> 00:07:14.095 align:start position:0%
Northern Italy became Europe's largest
theater of war.

00:07:12.295 --> 00:07:17.895 align:start position:0%
More and more of these foot soldiers
were mercenaries,

00:07:15.695 --> 00:07:22.995 align:start position:0%
who soon became indispensable to many
armies. The struggle for Novara in 1500

00:07:21.095 --> 00:07:27.895 align:start position:0%
offers a clear example.
Louis XII of France and Duke Ludovico

00:07:25.695 --> 00:07:32.395 align:start position:0%
Sforza were fighting for control of the
Duchy of Milan, and both employed

00:07:29.995 --> 00:07:36.295 align:start position:0%
several thousand Swiss mercenaries.
We don't know exactly what share of

00:07:34.095 --> 00:07:40.195 align:start position:0%
their armies these soldiers represented,
but it must have been substantial.

00:07:38.395 --> 00:07:45.595 align:start position:0%
When the Swiss in Milanese service
refused to fight their countrymen on the

00:07:42.195 --> 00:07:50.395 align:start position:0%
French side, Sforza had to surrender.
Without his mercenaries, his army was

00:07:47.695 --> 00:07:54.595 align:start position:0%
evidently unable to fight.
Other armies faced the same situation.

00:07:52.795 --> 00:07:59.295 align:start position:0%
As the Italian wars continued, the
numbers of Swiss and Landsknecht in

00:07:56.395 --> 00:08:04.295 align:start position:0%
foreign services rose even further.
At times, the French crown alone

00:08:01.195 --> 00:08:07.395 align:start position:0%
employed between 10,000 and 20,000 Swiss
mercenaries.

00:08:05.895 --> 00:08:11.297 align:start position:0%
Further developments in infantry
warfare, especially the emergence of

00:08:09.597 --> 00:08:15.297 align:start position:0%
pike and shot, helped to drive this
process.

00:08:12.997 --> 00:08:20.497 align:start position:0%
One catalyst was the French combination
of Swiss infantry and heavy cavalry,

00:08:18.097 --> 00:08:24.097 align:start position:0%
their famous gendarmes.
Together they formed a force that

00:08:22.197 --> 00:08:28.397 align:start position:0%
dominated the battlefields in the early
years of the Italian wars. The Spanish

00:08:26.797 --> 00:08:32.697 align:start position:0%
were among those who experienced
firsthand just how effective this

00:08:30.697 --> 00:08:35.697 align:start position:0%
combination could be when they fought
the French for control of the Kingdom of

00:08:34.397 --> 00:08:40.497 align:start position:0%
Naples.
At the Battle of Seminara in 1495,

00:08:38.397 --> 00:08:45.097 align:start position:0%
French men-at-arms and Swiss infantry
swept aside the traditional Spanish foot

00:08:42.497 --> 00:08:48.897 align:start position:0%
soldiers, most of them were spearmen or
sword and shield troops known as

00:08:47.097 --> 00:08:52.597 align:start position:0%
rodeleros.
The Spanish responded to their defeat at

00:08:50.497 --> 00:08:56.197 align:start position:0%
Seminara with far-reaching military
reforms.

00:08:53.897 --> 00:08:59.797 align:start position:0%
The famous general Gonzalo Fernández de
Córdoba, better known as El Gran

00:08:58.497 --> 00:09:04.197 align:start position:0%
Capitán,
reorganized the Spanish troops. He too

00:09:02.297 --> 00:09:09.497 align:start position:0%
increased the proportion of pikemen, but
unlike the Swiss, he organized them into

00:09:06.697 --> 00:09:13.697 align:start position:0%
smaller mobile units. Perhaps most
importantly, he equipped some of his

00:09:11.497 --> 00:09:17.697 align:start position:0%
troops with arquebuses. His new
formations combined the defensive

00:09:15.397 --> 00:09:20.697 align:start position:0%
strength of the pike with the firepower
of the arquebus.

00:09:19.097 --> 00:09:24.597 align:start position:0%
This combination was crucial to their
effectiveness because the two arms could

00:09:22.797 --> 00:09:28.497 align:start position:0%
cover each other's greatest weakness in
battle. The pikemen protected the

00:09:26.697 --> 00:09:32.197 align:start position:0%
arquebusiers from direct cavalry
attacks, while the arquebusiers

00:09:30.397 --> 00:09:36.297 align:start position:0%
prevented enemy troops from firing on
the pikemen on the pikes.

00:09:34.397 --> 00:09:40.297 align:start position:0%
Córdoba's new formations are considered
the direct predecessors of the famous

00:09:38.297 --> 00:09:44.597 align:start position:0%
Spanish tercios.
The reformed Spanish infantry soon

00:09:42.197 --> 00:09:49.297 align:start position:0%
proved its worth in battle, and other
infantry forces began following its

00:09:46.497 --> 00:09:53.097 align:start position:0%
example. The landsknechts, among others,
started incorporating firearms into

00:09:51.397 --> 00:09:56.797 align:start position:0%
their formations.
This made the infantry even more

00:09:54.597 --> 00:10:02.097 align:start position:0%
effective and cemented its increasingly
central role in warfare.

00:09:59.497 --> 00:10:08.297 align:start position:0%
And as infantry grew in importance, so
did mercenaries who filled its ranks.

00:10:05.197 --> 00:10:11.498 align:start position:0%
The Battle of Pavia in 1525 showed what
mercenaries fighting in this new way

00:10:10.298 --> 00:10:15.198 align:start position:0%
could achieve.
Emperor Charles the Fifth's pike and

00:10:13.098 --> 00:10:19.098 align:start position:0%
shot formations, made up mainly of
Landsknechts and Spanish infantry,

00:10:17.398 --> 00:10:23.898 align:start position:0%
inflicted a crushing defeat on the
French gendarmes. In the thick morning

00:10:21.498 --> 00:10:28.498 align:start position:0%
fog on February 24th,
the two sides unexpectedly came face to

00:10:26.198 --> 00:10:32.198 align:start position:0%
face outside the French camp.
The gendarmes drove off the enemy

00:10:29.998 --> 00:10:36.298 align:start position:0%
cavalry, only to find themselves caught
in the crossfire of imperial pike and

00:10:33.898 --> 00:10:39.298 align:start position:0%
shot infantry deployed along the edge of
a wood.

00:10:37.498 --> 00:10:42.598 align:start position:0%
The French men-at-arms tried to charge
the infantry as well,

00:10:40.898 --> 00:10:47.098 align:start position:0%
but whenever they moved to break through
the firing line, the musketeers withdrew

00:10:44.998 --> 00:10:52.298 align:start position:0%
behind the pikemen whose long pikes
forced the French cavalry to turn away.

00:10:49.898 --> 00:10:56.398 align:start position:0%
Under sustained fire, France's elite
cavalry was gradually worn down.

00:10:54.698 --> 00:10:59.998 align:start position:0%
The victory carried enormous symbolic
weight well beyond the battlefield of

00:10:58.498 --> 00:11:03.298 align:start position:0%
Pavia itself.
It left little doubt that this new kind

00:11:01.798 --> 00:11:06.598 align:start position:0%
of infantry now ruled [music] the
battlefield.

00:11:04.898 --> 00:11:09.498 align:start position:0%
By then, the role of mercenaries in
European armies had changed

00:11:08.198 --> 00:11:13.798 align:start position:0%
dramatically.
Historian David Parrott sums up the

00:11:11.298 --> 00:11:18.498 align:start position:0%
change. These developments, quote, turn
mercenaries from secondary force

00:11:15.698 --> 00:11:23.198 align:start position:0%
enhancers, such as Genoese crossbowmen,
into the dominant weapon system of the

00:11:20.698 --> 00:11:27.398 align:start position:0%
early 16th century.
Step by step, large formations of

00:11:25.198 --> 00:11:31.598 align:start position:0%
professional infantry displaced the
heavily armored cavalry of the nobility

00:11:29.598 --> 00:11:34.598 align:start position:0%
as the key element of warfare. To be
clear,

00:11:32.698 --> 00:11:37.898 align:start position:0%
military developments were only part of
this process.

00:11:35.998 --> 00:11:42.198 align:start position:0%
Different systems of recruitment and
organization continued to exist side by

00:11:40.198 --> 00:11:47.298 align:start position:0%
side for a long time.
Neither local levies nor heavy cavalry

00:11:44.898 --> 00:11:51.198 align:start position:0%
simply vanished from the battlefield.
What changed was the makeup of armies

00:11:49.598 --> 00:11:54.698 align:start position:0%
and the balance between their different
elements.

00:11:52.398 --> 00:11:58.398 align:start position:0%
Infantry became increasingly central,
and much of that infantry consisted of

00:11:56.598 --> 00:12:02.998 align:start position:0%
mercenaries.
The figures make the scale of this

00:11:59.898 --> 00:12:06.598 align:start position:0%
change clear. By the 1540s, mercenaries
made up more than half of the troops in

00:12:04.898 --> 00:12:12.198 align:start position:0%
many European armies.
In 1558, they accounted for a full 70%

00:12:10.333 --> 00:12:17.099 align:start position:0%
[music] of the French Royal Army.
And as David Parrott stresses, France

00:12:14.299 --> 00:12:21.499 align:start position:0%
was far from alone. Almost all Europe's
major rulers showed, in his words,

00:12:19.499 --> 00:12:27.199 align:start position:0%
quote, an escalating commitment to
expensive mercenary forces, end quote.

00:12:24.699 --> 00:12:32.199 align:start position:0%
But for all their advantages, mercenary
armies had two major drawbacks. Their

00:12:29.699 --> 00:12:35.499 align:start position:0%
soldiers were expensive and difficult to
control.

00:12:33.399 --> 00:12:39.199 align:start position:0%
Europe's rulers have faced the challenge
of paying, supplying,

00:12:37.299 --> 00:12:43.499 align:start position:0%
and controlling thousands of men who
fought primarily for money.

00:12:41.699 --> 00:12:47.399 align:start position:0%
We'll look at how they dealt with that
problem in another video.

